21:33:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right: no human has ever landed on another planet. The only world beyond Earth that people have visited is the Moon. The main reason is that the Moon is vastly closer and easier to reach than any planet: - **Moon:** about 384,000 km away; Apollo crews took about 3 days to get there. - **Mars:** typically tens to hundreds of millions of km away; a crewed trip would take roughly 6–9 months each way with current technology, plus time waiting for the right return opportunity. - **Venus:** closer at times, but its surface is lethally hot—around 465°C—with crushing atmospheric pressure. - **Mercury:** extremely difficult to reach and deal with because of the Sun’s gravity and extreme temperatures. - **Jupiter, Saturn, etc.:** far away, intensely radioactive or cold, and mostly have no solid surface to stand on. A human Mars mission also needs much more than a robot lander: - enough food, water, oxygen, and spare parts for years; - protection from solar storms and long-term cosmic radiation; - reliable medical care with no quick rescue possible; - a spacecraft that can land a large crew safely, then launch them back off Mars; - a way to return safely to Earth. Apollo was possible because the Moon is nearby and because the United States spent an enormous amount—driven largely by Cold War competition with the Soviet Union. After Apollo, governments did not continue funding similarly ambitious crewed missions at that scale. So it’s not that people *cannot* land on a planet. Mars is physically possible, and agencies and companies are working toward it. It just remains far more expensive, dangerous, and technically demanding than landing on the Moon.
