21:33:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: In the strict sense, yes: a crewed landing on Mars has **not been demonstrated**, so nobody can honestly say it has been proven in practice. When scientists and engineers say “it’s possible,” they usually mean: - no known law of physics prevents it; - many necessary pieces have been demonstrated separately—human spaceflight, long-duration stays in orbit, robotic Mars landings, life-support systems, rockets, rendezvous and docking, and returning crews from the Moon; - calculations and tests suggest those pieces could be combined into a Mars mission. But combining them is the hard part. A human Mars landing would involve risks and problems that have never all been solved together: years-long life support, radiation exposure, landing a much heavier vehicle than any Mars lander so far, maintaining equipment without rescue, and getting the crew home. So the careful statement is: **human landings on Mars are technologically plausible, but unproven and not yet achieved.** It is not an accomplished fact, and success is not guaranteed until it is actually done. Also, technically, humans have landed on a planet many times—we are on Earth—but the first landing by humans on a planet other than Earth has not happened.
