21:34:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That skepticism is understandable. Dates for human Mars missions have often been more like **aspirational goals** than funded, approved commitments. The pattern happens because a Mars landing needs several things to line up at once: - sustained political support over decades; - very large budgets; - tested heavy-lift launch systems and Mars landing hardware; - solutions for radiation, life support, fuel, and return; - willingness to accept substantial risk. Those conditions have not all existed together since Apollo. Agencies announce target dates partly to guide research and justify development, but a target date is not the same as a mission that has hardware built, funded, and ready to fly. So “it’s definitely going to happen by year X” is not justified. It may keep slipping, or humanity may decide the cost and risk are not worth it. The defensible claim is narrower: a human Mars mission is not forbidden by physics and appears technically conceivable, but there is currently no guaranteed, locked-in path to making it happen.
