21:34:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Distance is the core problem—but it creates most of the other problems rather than replacing them. Mars is not “too far” in the sense that a spacecraft cannot get there. Robots routinely do, and the physics of sending people there is understood. But it is far enough that: - travel takes many months, so crews need long-lasting food, water, air, power, repairs, and medical capability; - they spend much longer outside Earth’s magnetic protection, increasing radiation risk; - communication delays mean Earth cannot help in real time; - a rescue mission is essentially impossible; - returning requires planning around orbital windows and carrying or making fuel. For the Moon, a crew is only a few days away, can communicate almost instantly, and can return quickly in an emergency. Mars turns every failure into a potentially fatal, months-long problem. So radiation and life support are not separate “excuses”; they are direct consequences of the distance and travel time. Whether that makes a crewed Mars mission practically worthwhile is a political and economic question, not a question already settled by physics.
