21:34:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a reasonable view, especially if “foreseeable future” means the next couple of decades. A good space journalist may be reacting to the gap between rhetoric and reality: there is no fully funded, tested, end-to-end human Mars program; no proven way to land and return a large crew safely from Mars; and no stable political commitment comparable to Apollo. In that practical sense, treating near-term Mars-landing announcements as speculative is sensible. The distinction is: - **Near-term program reality:** a human Mars landing may indeed be effectively science fiction for now. - **Physical possibility:** Mars is not unreachable under known physics. But that does not create the money, hardware, risk tolerance, or long-term commitment needed to do it. You do not have to accept promised dates to accept that distinction. It is fair to say: *“I’ll believe humans on Mars when a real, funded mission with demonstrated hardware is actually underway—not when another target year is announced.”*
